[tool] d-03 post_finding {"claim": "Viel et al. 2005 (astro-ph/0501562) Eq. (5), checked against the full text: m_sterile = 4.43 keV (m_thermal/1 keV)^{4/3} (0.25(0.7)^2/omega_x)^{1/3}.
[finding] f-010 Viel et al. 2005 (astro-ph/0501562) Eq. (5), checked against the full text: m_sterile = 4.43 keV (m_thermal/1 keV)^{4/3} (0.25(0.7)^2/omega_x)^{1/3}. The coeffi
**Finding:** Viel et al. 2005 (astro-ph/0501562) Eq. (5), checked against the full text: m_sterile = 4.43 keV (m_thermal/1 keV)^{4/3} (0.25(0.7)^2/omega_x)^{1/3}. The coefficient 4.43 keV and the exponents 4/3 and -1/3 are confirmed, and 0.25*0.49 = 0.1225 matches the board's normalisation. The paper derives it by 'equating omega_x and m_x/T_x' of a thermal relic and a sterile neutrino 'which share the same phase-space distribution but with an overall suppression factor chi'. So it treats the DW spectrum as a rescaled Fermi-Dirac distribution with T_x = T_nu, and the two transfer functions are identical by construction.
[tool] tc-261019_1-w2-d-03-95 -> ok: Recorded finding f-010.
[tool] d-03 post_finding {"claim": "The Viel 2005 transfer-function fit, checked against the full text, is T(k) = [1+(alpha k)^{2nu}]^{-5/nu} with nu = 1.12 (best fit for k < 5 h/Mpc). 
[finding] f-011 The Viel 2005 transfer-function fit, checked against the full text, is T(k) = [1+(alpha k)^{2nu}]^{-5/nu} with nu = 1.12 (best fit for k < 5 h/Mpc). For thermal
**Finding:** The Viel 2005 transfer-function fit, checked against the full text, is T(k) = [1+(alpha k)^{2nu}]^{-5/nu} with nu = 1.12 (best fit for k < 5 h/Mpc). For thermal relics alpha = 0.049 (m_x/1 keV)^{-1.11} (Omega_x/0.25)^{0.11} (h/0.7)^{1.22} h^-1 Mpc; the general form is alpha = 0.24 ((m_x/T_x)/(1 keV/T_nu))^{-0.83} (omega_x/0.1225)^{-0.16} Mpc. The half-mode condition T^2 = 1/2 gives k_hm = (2^{nu/10}-1)^{1/(2nu)}/alpha = 0.325/alpha (my own arithmetic). Taking Omega_x = 0.25 and h = 0.7, the thermal limits give alpha = 0.01396, 0.01302, 0.01024 and 0.00710 h^-1 Mpc, so k_hm is about 23.3, 25.0, 31.8 and 45.8 h/Mpc for 3.1, 3.3, 4.1 and 5.7 keV. Under Viel's mapping the DW k_hm at the mapped mass is identical by construction.
[tool] tc-261019_1-w2-d-03-96 -> ok: Recorded finding f-011.
[tool] d-03 post_finding {"claim": "DW mass floors under the verified Viel 2005 Eq. (5) with omega_dm = 0.12, for which the factor (0.1225/0.12)^{1/3} = 1.0069 (hand arithmetic): 3.1 ke
[finding] f-012 DW mass floors under the verified Viel 2005 Eq. (5) with omega_dm = 0.12, for which the factor (0.1225/0.12)^{1/3} = 1.0069 (hand arithmetic): 3.1 keV gives 3.1
**Finding:** DW mass floors under the verified Viel 2005 Eq. (5) with omega_dm = 0.12, for which the factor (0.1225/0.12)^{1/3} = 1.0069 (hand arithmetic): 3.1 keV gives 3.1^{4/3} = 4.520, times 4.43 = 20.02, times 1.0069 = 20.2 keV. 3.3 keV gives 4.913, 21.77 and 21.9 keV. 4.1 keV gives 6.562, 29.07 and 29.3 keV. 5.7 keV gives 10.18, 45.11 and 45.4 keV. With omega = 0.1225 exactly, the values are 20.0, 21.8, 29.1 and 45.1 keV. This confirms the board's rough figures of 20, 22, 29 and 45 keV to within 1%.
[tool] tc-261019_1-w2-d-03-97 -> ok: Recorded finding f-012.
[tool] d-03 post_finding {"claim": "The general nCDM framework of 2007.13751 (Murgia et al. form) uses T(k) = [1+(alpha k)^beta]^gamma at z = 99, which reduces to the thermal WDM fit wh
[finding] f-013 The general nCDM framework of 2007.13751 (Murgia et al. form) uses T(k) = [1+(alpha k)^beta]^gamma at z = 99, which reduces to the thermal WDM fit when beta = 2
**Finding:** The general nCDM framework of 2007.13751 (Murgia et al. form) uses T(k) = [1+(alpha k)^beta]^gamma at z = 99, which reduces to the thermal WDM fit when beta = 2nu, gamma = -5/nu and nu = 1.12. A 2 keV WDM is fitted by [alpha, beta, gamma] = [0.02, 2.2, -4.5], with priors alpha in [0, 0.1], beta in [1, 10] and gamma in [-10, 0]. In the sections read, the paper gives no DW-specific relation between thermal-relic and sterile mass; it is applied to ultra-light axions. Using it for DW would require fitting (alpha, beta, gamma) to a Boltzmann-code DW transfer function.
