[direction] d-03 -> blocked (The tool budget ran out before an alternative mapping could be read and checked against the full text, so the spread between mappings and the <p/T> check could not be done.)
**Blocked — Cross-check the mapping from thermal-relic mass to DW sterile-neutrino mass. Verify the Viel et al. 2005 formula and its coefficient against the full text, gather alternative mappings (half-mode matching, the 2007.13751 framework, Abazajian-style relations), and quantify how much the DW mass floors change between mappings..** (The tool budget ran out before an alternative mapping could be read and checked against the full text, so the spread between mappings and the <p/T> check could not be done.) Two of the three success criteria are not met: there is no table under a second mapping, so no spread between mappings, and the DW <p/T> check was not done. What was established: (1) The Viel et al. 2005 relation is checked against the full text (f-010; source text at /Users/nramachandra/hep-genesis-testing/projects/ramp-research-lyman-wdm/runs/261019_110120/astro-ph_0501562.txt). Eq. (5) reads m_sterile = 4.43 keV (m_thermal/1 keV)^{4/3} (0.25(0.7)^2/omega_x)^{1/3}, which confirms the coefficient 4.43 keV and the exponents 4/3 and -1/3. The paper gets it by equating omega_x and m_x/T_x, treating the DW spectrum as a rescaled Fermi-Dirac distribution, so the thermal and DW transfer functions are identical by construction. (2) Its fitting form is T(k) = [1+(alpha k)^{2nu}]^{-5/nu} with nu = 1.12, fitted for k < 5 h/Mpc. For thermal relics alpha = 0.049 (m_x/keV)^{-1.11} (Omega_x/0.25)^{0.11} (h/0.7)^{1.22} h^-1 Mpc. By my own arithmetic k_hm = 0.325/alpha, which gives about 23.3, 25.0, 31.8 and 45.8 h/Mpc for 3.1, 3.3, 4.1 and 5.7 keV. These lie outside the k range the fit was calibrated on, and DW k_hm is identical under this mapping (f-011). (3) My own arithmetic gives DW floors, under Viel with omega_dm = 0.12, of 20.2, 21.9, 29.3 and 45.4 keV; with omega = 0.1225 they are 20.0, 21.8, 29.1 and 45.1 keV (f-012). (4) The framework in 2007.13751 is T(k) = [1+(alpha k)^beta]^gamma. It reduces to the Viel form when beta = 2nu and gamma = -5/nu, and a 2 keV WDM is fitted by [0.02, 2.2, -4.5]. In the sections read it gives no DW-specific relation (f-013). Which mapping to use with d-01: Viel Eq. (5), because it is the convention behind the board's floors. This is a provisional recommendation: other mappings may shift the floors, but no second mapping has been checked. Not done: a second mapping (Abazajian astro-ph/0512631 was downloaded to the run directory but not read; the Bozek/Murgia half-mode relations were not found), the spread between mappings, the DW <p/T> of about 3.15 T_nu, the comparison with the tool's free-streaming length, and cite() entries in references.bib. See dead end x-03 and question q-08.
[usage]
[direction] d-05 -> blocked (The X-ray f_s,max column cannot be filled because neither d-04 nor the tool gives numerical sin^2 2theta X-ray limits at 7.1-45 keV (dead end x-04). The quotes from both DW papers still need validating with cite(), which was not run because the budget ran out.)
**Blocked — Cross-check the sterile-nu server's Dodelson-Widrow relic-abundance normalisation against at least two quote-verified published DW yield formulas. Propagate the spread to sin^2 2theta(f_s=1) at 7.1, 20, 22, 29 and 45 keV. Then assemble the final comparison table: allowed DW fraction versus sterile mass from Lyman-alpha mixed-DM bounds and from X-ray limits, with the binding constraint marked at each mass..** (The X-ray f_s,max column cannot be filled because neither d-04 nor the tool gives numerical sin^2 2theta X-ray limits at 7.1-45 keV (dead end x-04). The quotes from both DW papers still need validating with cite(), which was not run because the budget ran out.) Comparison of the DW normalisation (finding f-014). The tool's CSV rows (sterile_nu_grid_DW_a74034.csv, tc-...-78) give Omega_s h^2 at sin^2 2theta = 1e-12 of 0.000471462 (7.1 keV), 0.00304114 (20 keV), 0.00361029 (22 keV), 0.00593606 (29 keV) and 0.0130907 (45 keV). Scaling linearly gives sin^2 2theta(f_s=1) = 2.55e-10, 3.95e-11, 3.32e-11, 2.02e-11 and 9.17e-12.

Two published DW formulas were read in the full texts and quoted verbatim in the findings, but cite() was not run because the budget ran out:
- Abazajian 2006 (astro-ph/0511630), Eq. 7: m_s = 3.40 keV (sin^2 2theta/1e-8)^-0.615 (Omega_DM/0.26)^0.5, for T_QCD = 170 MeV and electron flavour. Inverted, it gives 3.02e-9, 5.61e-10, 4.80e-10, 3.06e-10 and 1.50e-10 at the five masses. That is 11.9, 14.2, 14.4, 15.2 and 16.4 times the tool's values.
- Asaka-Laine-Shaposhnikov (hep-ph/0612182, Sec. 7.1), 'most conservative' line: log10 sin^2 2theta = -7.6195 - 1.7428 log10(M/keV). It gives 7.88e-10, 1.30e-10, 1.10e-10, 6.79e-11 and 3.16e-11, which is 3.10 to 3.44 times the tool's values. The paper states this fit for M <= 4 keV, so using it here is an extrapolation. Because it is the overclosure upper line, the factor of about 3 is a lower limit.

So the tool produces about 3 to 16 times more DW dark matter per unit mixing than the published fits. This agrees with the critic's estimate of a factor of about 3, and it is larger when measured against Abazajian. All of this arithmetic is mine.

At the 3.5 keV line point (7.1 keV, sin^2 2theta = 7e-11), f_s = 0.275 with the tool, 0.089 with ALS and 0.023 with Abazajian (f-015). The tool is the most generous of the three, so its X-ray exclusion of f_s = 1 at 20 keV becomes stronger under the published normalisations.

Combined table (f-016, low confidence). It uses the 0812.0010 bound F_wdm < 0.35 (quote-verified), the 2504.06367 bounds mapped to DW mass with the unverified Viel formula (my estimate), and the Viel-mapped pure-DW floors (unverified).
- 7.1 keV: Lyman-alpha mixed-DM is binding, f_s <~ 0.2-0.35.
- 20 and 22 keV: Lyman-alpha gives f <~ 0.5-0.67. The tool's X-ray check excludes f_s = 1 at 20 keV.
- 29 keV: f = 1 is allowed by Lyman-alpha only for the 4.1 keV variant.
- 45 keV: f = 1 is allowed by Lyman-alpha for the 5.7 keV variant.
- 29 and 45 keV: the X-ray limit is presumably binding, but no numerical X-ray value exists.

This answers q-04: the floor drops from about 45 keV (5.7 keV thermal limit) to about 29 keV (4.1 keV thermal limit).

The brief's warning that the tool lacks Omega_s h^2 is out of date: the tool does return Omega_s h^2, so f_s is tool-derived.

Full texts are saved as astro-ph_0511630.txt and hep-ph_0612182.txt in /Users/nramachandra/hep-genesis-testing/projects/ramp-research-lyman-wdm/runs/261019_110120/.
